 marker's ownership as well. The bits reach `mknod`, so the process umask
 reduces them in both implementations, and a checkout outside `-U` then applies
 the recorded mode in full, after the ownership: a chown on a device node clears
 the setuid and setgid bits, which the cited test states with a 04755 and a
 02755 marker. The marker's extended attributes are applied ahead of both, which
 a `user.` attribute turns into a refusal on both sides;
 `format-reference.md`, "Checkout" states that rule and the library test
 `ostrya::checkout::passthrough_whiteout_xattrs_reach_the_device` holds it,
 since a CLI-level case would need a tool the suite does not carry to write the
 attribute.

