evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::remote_refs_and_summary_match_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#remote
note: reading a live remote needs a server, which no setup binds. The test serves
 a repository over HTTP and compares `remote refs`, `refs -r`, the summary
 report, `--raw`, `--list-metadata-keys`, three `--print-metadata-key` forms, and
 the refusal a remote publishing no summary draws. The timestamps in the report
 are the one divergence: the tool renders them in the host time zone and the port
 in UTC, so the test fixes `TZ=UTC` (`cli-surface.md`, "P3").

family: M10
subcommand: remote
cell: remote/gpg-forms
setup: repo
outcome: lossy
severity: interop
loss: `gpg-list-keys` omits the two Web Key Directory URL lines the tool prints
 per user id, the `Subkey:` line it prints per subkey, and the `(revoked)`
 marker it prints after a revoked key's fingerprint and after each of its
 revoked user ids, and renders the key's
 creation instant in UTC rather than the host locale and time zone. The
 fingerprint, the creation instant, and the user ids are the same in both. The
 keyring `gpg-import` writes carries no Trust packet, where the tool's carries
 one after the primary key packet, after each user id packet, and after each
 signature packet; with those packets dropped the two hold
 the same packets in the same order, and each implementation reads the keyring the
 other wrote. A `KEY-ID` is read as the dialect `gpg --export` carries, and each
 implementation takes a key the other does not. A certificate offered for a key
 the keyring already holds leaves that key as the keyring holds it, where the tool
 merges the offered material into it, so a new user id, a new subkey, and a subkey
 revocation reach the port's trusted set only after `remote delete` and a fresh
 import. Two statements are carried in by both. A key revocation that verifies
 replaces the held certificate in the port's keyring and is merged into it in the
 tool's, and each implementation then refuses a signature that key made: the key
 itself signs the revocation, or a key a verified self-signature designates as a
 revoker does, and the port resolves such a revoker among the certificates of the
 keyring under edit and of the offered stream. Two states reach the tool's
 keyring and not the port's, and the port then reports a good signature over a
 key the tool reports revoked: a revoker whose certificate stands in the global
 trusted directory rather than in either stream, where both implementations
 report a good signature and the bytes alone part, and a revoker imported after
 the revocation was offered. A key
 expiry later than the held certificate states replaces it the same way, and each
 implementation then reports a good signature where it reported a bad one before;
 the port's keyring is the offered export byte for byte and the tool's merge
 keeps the packets the earlier export carried, so the two files part by more than
 the Trust packets. A certificate carrying no user id is taken by the port and
 refused by the tool (`cli-surface.md`, "P3", states them all).
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::remote_gpg_keyring_round_trips_with_the_tool,
 ostrya_cli::cli::remote_gpg_import_carries_a_key_expiry_extension,
 ostrya_cli::cli::remote_gpg_import_carries_a_designated_revokers_revocation,
 ostrya_cli::cli::remote_gpg_import_leaves_an_unverifiable_revocation_out
spec: format-reference.md#remote
note: importing keys needs a generated key and a remote, which no setup binds.
 The test imports one keyring into each implementation's repository and states
 that each reads the keyring the other wrote, that the count a repeated import
 reports is the same, that a `KEY-ID` selection takes the key it names, that the
 imported key verifies a commit the tool's own `gpg-sign` signed with it, and
 that deleting the remote takes its keyring with it. `gpg-list-keys` is the
 divergence the outcome names:
 the port reports the fingerprint, the creation instant, and the user ids, and
 leaves out the two Web Key Directory URL lines, the per-subkey `Subkey:` line,
 and the `(revoked)` marker, rendering the instant in UTC rather than the host
 locale. The revoked re-export of a held key and a re-export stating a later
 expiry are the two offered certificates that change a keyring the count reports
 nothing for; the keyring the port rewrites for the revocation is the one
 `ostree remote gpg-list-keys` reads and reports `(revoked)`, and `ostree show`
 reports `Key revoked` over it. The second test carries the expiry extension
 through both implementations over a commit each signed with the key while it
 was live. The third and the fourth carry a revocation a designated revoker
 made: the third states the two keyrings that resolve the revoker out of the
 streams the import reads, and the fourth the two that do not
 (`cli-surface.md`, "P3").

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# commit -- the message and the body, the metadata dict, and the ref bindings
# (Phase 17f). Every cell here states a timestamp, so `checksum-agreement` is
# the oracle: the subject, the body, the metadata dict, and the entry order of
# that dict are all commit-object fields, and one checksum on both sides states
# them together.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: commit
cell: commit/body-inline
setup: repo tree
run: commit --repo=$REPO -b conformance -s subj -m 'body text' --timestamp=@1700000000 $TREE
expect-exit: 0
expect-stderr: empty
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
