 implementations compile the value with PCRE2: the tool through GLib's
 `GRegex`, and the port through the `pcre2` crate's vendored 10.46. Both carry
 the anchors, `\b` and `\B`, the POSIX class names, the character and Unicode
 escapes, every group form, the greedy, lazy, and possessive quantifiers, the
 inline options, the four lookarounds, the atomic and comment groups,
 `\Q...\E`, `\C`, `\K`, backreferences, recursion, subroutine calls,
 conditionals, `\h`, `\R`, `\N`, `\g{1}`, octal escapes, `(?J)`, `(?x)`, a
 callout, the backtracking verbs, and the start-of-pattern options; each form
 the cited test holds reaches the tool's commit checksum. `(?J)` is held on
 both halves of the value: the expression puts one name on two groups, and the
 replacement reads that name back with `\g<name>`, which resolves to the first
 group of that name the match set. Measured in both directions, no expression
 both compile is answered differently, and every expression one refuses the
 other refuses too. The residue is the reason string in a compile-failure line,
 the unit that same line's offset counts in, and the exit path a match-time
 refusal takes, all three outside the oracle (`cli-surface.md`, "Scope of CLI
 compatibility"), and the PCRE2 version, which the port vendors and the tool
 takes from the host GLib.

