 tool stores a name-dependent order over 6 of 40 generated names
 (`cli-surface.md`, "P2"). Every reader looks a key up by name and
 `show --list-detached-metadata-keys` sorts, so the two listings agree, and the
 commit object each writes is the same, which `commit/gpg-sign` above states.
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::commit_gpg_sign_matches_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#signing-details
note: the port stores insertion order -- the caller's detached-metadata keys in
 command-line order, then `ostree.sign.<type>`, then `ostree.gpgsigs` --
 whatever order the signing options take on the command line, and `show
 --list-detached-metadata-keys` sorts them instead, which the cited test states
 by reading the listing and the stored order of one commit and finding them
 different. The test reads the key order out of the raw `.commitmeta`, over both
 option orders, three user-key names, and a run carrying two user keys. It holds
 the two stored orders against each other over `zzz` and `zzy`, the names where
 they meet, and over the rest requires them to differ, so the divergence the
 `loss` field states cannot go stale.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Abbreviated checksum resolution, Phase 17f (`X1` and `F10a`).
#
# A revision shorter than a full checksum names the one commit object whose
# checksum starts with it (`../format-reference.md`, "Revision syntax"). Every
# case rests on a prefix of a checksum, which no placeholder holds a substring
# of, so these cells cite `evidence:` and the tests they name derive the prefix
# from the checksum the two implementations printed for the same fixed-timestamp
# commit. A prefix placeholder bound by `repo-with-commit` would turn the
# single-invocation cases into `run:` cells; that is a harness change.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

family: M10
subcommand: rev-parse
cell: rev-parse/abbreviated-checksum-lengths
modes: archive
setup: repo tree
outcome: full
severity: interop
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::abbreviated_checksum_resolves_like_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#object-store-layout
note: a prefix of the one commit resolves to it at one, two, three, four, eight,
 32, and 63 characters, and the whole 64 resolve as they did before. One
 character past a checksum, and an uppercase rendering of a prefix, are ref names
 naming nothing in both. The cited test compares `rev-parse` for each length
 against the tool over one repository per implementation.

