 The cited test states the tool's refusal text and holds the port's
 trailing-slash archive to its own `--subpath=/dir` archive byte for byte.

family: M10
subcommand: export
cell: export/output-opens-before-the-revision-resolves
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: lossy
severity: interop
loss: what the destination holds after a refusal. Both implementations open
 the destination before the revision resolves, so both truncate a destination
 that already existed and keep its inode and its mode, and neither writes an
 archive into it. The tool then leaves the two trailing zero blocks of the
 archive it had opened, all NUL -- 1024 bytes into a `-o` destination, and 10240
 onto standard output, where those blocks are padded up to one record -- and the
 port leaves nothing (`cli-surface.md`, "P2").
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::export_output_switch_matches_the_tool
spec: cli-surface.md#p2----options-missing-from-commands-that-exist
note: the case needs a destination that already exists, which no setup binds.
 The cited test writes content into the destination, sets its mode to 0640,
 runs `export -o <dest> no-such-ref`, and holds each side to exit 1, to the
 inode and the mode the destination had, to the size that side leaves, and to
 a content of NUL bytes alone. It states the same over standard output, where
 a refused revision and a subpath naming nothing both leave the tool's
 10240 bytes and the port's none.

family: M10
subcommand: export
cell: export/member-order
setup: repo-with-commit
outcome: lossy
severity: interop
loss: the member order, and with it which path of a content-sharing group is
 written in full. The tool emits a directory's non-directory entries in name
 order and then its subdirectories in name order, each subdirectory's own
 members following it; the port emits every entry of a directory in one name
 order. The first member the walk reaches for a content object is the one
 written in full, so a group whose paths lie in one directory coalesces the same
 way in both and a group spanning two directories coalesces in opposite
 directions. A hardlink's own header carries the linked-to member's metadata in
 the tool and zeros in the port. The port emits one `SCHILY.xattr.*` record per
 stored xattr and the tool emits none (`../format-reference.md`, "tar"). Neither
 option in this section changes either side's order.
evidence: ostrya_cli::cli::export_options_match_the_tool,
 ostrya_cli::cli::export_hardlink_direction_parts_from_the_tool
spec: format-reference.md#tar
note: the order is a property of the stream and no oracle reads it. The first
 cited test holds each side's member order under every option to the order that
 side set in its option-free export, which is the claim that no option reorders
 a stream, and compares the extracted tree, where a hardlink reads the metadata
 and the content of the member it links to, so the direction of a coalesced
 group leaves the tree unchanged. The second exports a tree holding `/z.txt` and
 `/adir/a.txt` on one content object and states that the tool writes `z.txt` in
 full while the port writes `adir/a.txt` in full.

